{{smaller block|You have had a ''brisk'' time of it at Howick, and all the organs of combativeness have been called into action.
{{right|''Sydney Smith'', To the Countess Grey.}}}}
'''4.''' Burning freely; bright: as, a ''brisk'' fire.—'''5.''' Effervescing vigorously: said of liquors: as, ''brisk'' cider.—'''6.''' Performed or kept up with briskness; rapid; quick: as, a ''brisk'' fire of infantry.

{{smaller block|''Brisk'' toil alternating with ready ease. {{right|''Wordsworth.''}}}}
'''7†.''' Vivid; luminous.

{{smaller block|<poem>He hunts about the proudest World to buy
The choice of purest and of brightest Cloth
''Brisk'' in the Tyrian and Sidonian dye,
As due to his fair Darling.</poem>
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, i. 83.}}
Had it [my instrument] magnified thirty or twenty-five times, it had made the object appear more ''brisk'' and pleasant. {{right|''Newton.''}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 1.''' Alert, nimble, quick, rapid, sprightly, prompt, spry, smart, bustling, wide-awake, eager. See ''active'' and ''busy''.}}

'''brisk''' (brisk), ''v.'' [< ''brisk'', ''a.''] '''I.†''' ''trans.'' To make lively; enliven; animate; refresh: sometimes with ''up''. ''Killingbeck.''

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become brisk, lively, or active: with ''up''.

'''brisken''' (bris′kn), ''v.'' [< ''brisk'' + ''-en''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To be or become brisk, active, or lively. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|I heartily wish that business may ''brisken'' a little.
{{right|Quoted in ''W. Mathews’s'' Getting on in the World, p. 209.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To make brisk or lively.

