¶
'''bristle''' (bris′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''bristled'', ppr. ''bristling''. [< ME. ''bristlen'', ''brustlen'' (= G. ''börsteln''), bristle; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To erect the bristles of; erect in anger or defiance, as a hog erects its bristles.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now, for the bare-pick’d bone of majesty
Doth dogged war ''bristle'' his angry crest,
And snarleth in the gentle eyes of peace.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iv. 3.}}
Boy, ''bristle'' thy courage up. {{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' To make bristly.—'''3.''' To fix a bristle on: as, to ''bristle'' a shoemaker’s thread.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To rise up or stand on end like bristles.
{{smaller block|<poem>Nought dreadful saw he; yet the hair
’Gan ''bristle'' on his head with fear.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, iii. 42.}}}}
'''2.''' To stand erect and close together like bristles.
{{smaller block|A forest of masts would have ''bristled'' in the desolate port of Newry. {{right|''Macaulay''.}}}}
'''3.''' To be covered, as with bristles: as, the ranks ''bristled'' with spears. See ''to bristle with'', below.—'''To bristle against''', {{smaller|to come in collision with, contradict, or oppose somewhat rudely. [Rare.]}}
{{smaller block|The wife may not ''bristle against'' her husband.
{{right|''J. Udall'', On Ephesians, v.}}}}

